      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The optimization of mRNA delivery vectors hinges on advanced lipid nanoparticle (LNP) physicochemical engineering, specifically through the substitution of conventional ionizable lipids with sterically hindered, branched-tail amphiphiles. This structural modification directly elevates the phase transition temperature ($\\text{T}_\\text{m}$) of the lipid bilayer, thereby kinetically suppressing the hydrolytic degradation pathways of the encapsulated messenger RNA cargo. Furthermore, the strategic integration of highly saturated cholesterol analogues reinforces the hydrophobic core's lamellar organization, providing superior resistance against reactive oxygen species (ROS)-mediated oxidative damage to the phosphodiester backbone of the nucleotide sequences. This enhanced thermodynamic stability facilitates the development of lyophilization-compatible, shelf-stable mRNA formulations, obviating the stringent requirements of ultra-low temperature ($\\text{ULT}$) cryopreservation.",
      "source": "target",
